# Keep text files on LF line endings in every working tree.
# This avoids Windows/Linux/Docker churn and keeps scripts portable.
* text=auto eol=lf

# C# sources are text, ALWAYS — never left to auto-detection.
#
# `text=auto` above decides per file by sniffing content, and a single NUL byte makes it decide
# "binary": no textual diff, no textual merge, and `grep` silently declines to match. That is not
# hypothetical here. Two .cs files reached this repo carrying raw NUL bytes as sentinel characters
# (`"\0no-reviewbot-remote"`, and the mount dedication probe) written as literal bytes rather than
# as the C# escape `\0`. One of them was Workspace/ReviewSlot.cs, which grew ~10 KB landing the
# untrusted-PR mount gate — a security seam whose diff nobody could read, because git rendered the
# whole change as `Bin 3366 -> 13085 bytes`.
#
# Declaring the type here removes the sniffing step, so the same mistake stays reviewable instead of
# disappearing. It does not stop a NUL being committed; it stops one from hiding the change around it.
*.cs text eol=lf diff

# Scripts and hooks must remain LF for container and Unix execution.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Binary files should never be line-ending normalized.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.7z binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
